{
  "dom_id": "step-t5386",
  "name": "Deriving the Formula for the Full Surface Area of a Cylinder",
  "problem": "We now wish to find a formula for the total surface area of a right cylinder. To do this, let's consider a right cylinder of radius $r$ and height $h,$ as shown below. This time, we include the bases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268062/assets/d3320f15008796a4c49366ebe7f0933ac7d018aeefb212adca59fd134e656d3d.png)\n\nThe net of the cylinder is constructed from the lateral surface plus two congruent circular bases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268062/assets/02365f72f440f99f51d290c775017be62582be5742f0c3dfde0b545465b94754.png)\n\nLet $S_L$ be the lateral surface area of the cylinder, and let $S_B$ be the area of the bases. The surface area $S$ of the cylinder can be expressed\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2{S}_{B}+{S}_{L} \\\\  & =2(\\pi {r}^{2})+2\\pi r\\cdot h \\\\  & =2\\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
